# Art invariants

These were each paid for once. Do not rediscover them in GLSL, in a new painter, or in a "simpler" analyzer.

## Anatomy and species

- A dog is not a cat with different ears. Separate mesh, separate skeleton proportions, separate groom. Never parameterise one into the other.
- Dogs read as dogs from the skeleton: legs that stand them clear of the ground, a deep chest over a tucked waist, a topline sloping from shoulder to hip, hocked back legs.
- Tail sanity check on every new view. The side-view tail was invisible for months (origin inside the body capsule, sign error on `dir = -1`). Rig it, render walk and trot, and look for a tail.
- The profile view keeps a smaller, set-back far eye. Drawing only the near eye was read as the far eye being shut while walking.

## Shading and colour

- Shading keys off the whole silhouette, not each shape's edge. Per-shape distance puts a dark rim wherever a leg meets the body (inflated sausages). `silhouetteDepth` was the 2D fix; in 3D do not relit per-shell as if each were a separate object.
- Fur has a gamut: near-neutral, or warm between about 12° and 60°. Saturated greens are floors and cardboard; saturated pure reds are beds and cushions. Clamp incoming coat colour. This lives in the material, not only in the analyzer.
- A near-black coat must not read as a hole in a dark page. `SHADOW_FLOOR` in `engine/coat.js` was a channel-wise luminance floor; in 3D this is a tonemap shoulder, not a clamp-to-grey. Keep the behaviour, delete the hack.
- Muted irises. Saturated irises read as neon at any resolution.
- Two-layer coat: undercoat colour beneath guard-hair colour. Real fur does not look painted.

## Markings live in body coordinates

- Markings are bound to body space (along-body `u`, around-body `v`, region IDs), never screen space. This is `Part` and the region map, promoted to a UV atlas in 3D.
- A blaze is not "white". It is white up the middle with colour either side. Never use total white fraction. Measure the centre band against the flanks.
- No seam line around the muzzle. `['muzzle','head']` drew a line no cat has. In 3D that is a UV or normal-map seam — check it explicitly.

## Measurement (analyzer)

- k-means on RGB clusters a coat by brightness, not colour. Use `chromaClusters`: divide brightness out, take the 75th-percentile brightness per cluster, weight × (0.15 + sat)^1.5.
- Otsu is not enough to find white on a close-up. The threshold must also sit above the animal's own median luminance.
- Do not guess iris colour. Ginger cheek fur measures 0.66–0.83 saturation. Ask the person to click the eye.
- Asking beats classifying. Shape scoring put a photo of a cat's back in the face slot at 0.92 confidence. Keep guided capture (`analyzer/shots.js`) and "which one is looking at you?"
- `readFromShots` reads each parameter from the shot that can *see* it. Averaging every measurement over every photo is noise.
- Never use SAM's own confidence. It was 0.97 on a fridge. Score candidates by box fill minus spill.
- Bake EXIF orientation. Unbaked photos measure the wrong axis.

## Eyes, blinks, motion

- Blink with a floor: `0.42 + 0.58 * open`. Linear eye-height scaling collapses squints into dark smudges.
- Both eyes share a phase. One eye open and one shut was a real bug; in 3D it returns as two independently-driven blend shapes. Drive both from one value.
- Idle stillness is a feature. Port `stage.js` weights (sit 26, sleep 7, …). A pet that constantly performs is exhausting to have on screen.
- Foot slide is the most common realism break in web 3D animals. Measure it.

## Compositing and sizing

- Canvas wants straight alpha, CoreGraphics wants premultiplied. Backwards is a dark halo around the whole animal.
- No fixed grid. Draw at `cssPixels × devicePixelRatio` every frame. The 2D engine's pixelation was upscaling a capped cache, not a filter bug.

## Honest ceiling

- At overlay size (300–700px) a viewer should read a real animal. A 4K frozen close-up will not fool anyone. It is their breed and colouring, recognisable — not a forensic duplicate. Do not overpromise in copy or in a shader comment.
